WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.315
Say you need the fees for 2,000,000 ledger entries, and you have four workers.

2
00:00:07.533 --> 00:00:09.580
A RecursiveTask splits the job.

3
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:12.485
If its range is small enough, it just loops.

4
00:00:12.485 --> 00:00:15.852
If not, it cuts it in half, into two smaller tasks.

5
00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:19.574
The lesson's demo uses a threshold of 50,000.

6
00:00:19.574 --> 00:00:22.459
Six rounds of halving give 64 leaves.

7
00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:28.780
In code: fork the left half, compute the right half on this thread, then join the left half.

8
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.541
A ThreadPoolExecutor has one shared queue.

9
00:00:33.541 --> 00:00:38.173
A ForkJoinPool gives every worker its own deque: a queue with two ends.

10
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.022
The job starts on worker 1.

11
00:00:40.022 --> 00:00:46.328
Each split forks the left half onto the top of its own deque, and the worker goes on with the right half.

12
00:00:46.533 --> 00:00:48.190
It also pops from the top.

13
00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:53.480
So it works on its newest task: the smallest one, whose data is still in its cache.

14
00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.453
Worker 2 has nothing to do, so it steals.

15
00:00:56.453 --> 00:01:03.303
It takes the bottom task of worker 1's deque: the oldest one, forked highest up, so the biggest piece.

16
00:01:03.303 --> 00:01:04.579
Half the whole job.

17
00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.180
The thief splits it on its own deque, and stays busy for a long time.

18
00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:10.957
Workers 3 and 4 do the same.

19
00:01:11.167 --> 00:01:13.742
Three steals, and all four are busy.

20
00:01:13.742 --> 00:01:18.391
That's why the demo needs only a handful of steals for 64 leaves.

21
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.324
And join doesn't just park.

22
00:01:20.324 --> 00:01:23.644
If nobody stole the task, the worker runs it itself.

23
00:01:23.644 --> 00:01:28.113
If a thief has it, the worker runs other pending tasks while it waits.

24
00:01:29.033 --> 00:01:30.024
Now the order.

25
00:01:30.024 --> 00:01:33.069
Fork the left half, so a thief can take it.

26
00:01:33.069 --> 00:01:35.051
Compute the right half here.

27
00:01:35.051 --> 00:01:35.759
Then join.

28
00:01:35.967 --> 00:01:37.313
The halves overlap.

29
00:01:37.313 --> 00:01:42.273
On four free cores, the demo runs 3 to 4 times faster than one thread.

30
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:48.032
Swap the last two steps, and the worker waits for the left half before it even starts the right one.

31
00:01:48.233 --> 00:01:52.635
Usually it pops the left half and runs it itself, then the right half.

32
00:01:52.635 --> 00:01:54.899
At every split, one after the other.

33
00:01:54.899 --> 00:01:57.917
So the sum takes about as long as on one thread.

34
00:01:58.833 --> 00:02:03.332
left.compute() + right.compute() is the same thing, written honestly.

35
00:02:03.332 --> 00:02:07.636
All of them give the right total, so the bug survives code review.

36
00:02:07.867 --> 00:02:09.678
So: fork, compute, join.

37
00:02:09.678 --> 00:02:12.019
Or let invokeAll do it for you.
